{"content":[{"c":["HIVITES"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Name of a pre-Israelite group living in Canaan. Though not yet discovered/rated archaeologically or from secular history as a people, they were regarded as emerging from a son of Canaan (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.10.17","r":"Gen.10.17","rawR":"Gen.10.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.10.17","value":"Gen.10.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 10:17"},") and as inhabiting areas of the Lebanon Mountains (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.3.3","r":"Judg.3.3","rawR":"Judg.3.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.3.3","value":"Judg.3.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 3:3"},") and Mt Hermon (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.11.3","r":"Josh.11.3","rawR":"Josh.11.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.11.3","value":"Josh.11.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 11:3"},"). They are referred to frequently as a group dispossessed by Israel (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.12.8","r":"Josh.12.8","rawR":"Josh.12.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.12.8","value":"Josh.12.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 12:8"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.24.11","r":"Josh.24.11","rawR":"Josh.24.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.24.11","value":"Josh.24.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"24:11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.9.20","r":"1Kgs.9.20","rawR":"1Kgs.9.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.9.20","value":"1Kgs.9.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 9:20"},") but who managed to survive into the kingdom period (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.24.7","r":"2Sam.24.7","rawR":"2Sam.24.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.24.7","value":"2Sam.24.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 24:7"},") and lived at that time near Tyre as well as in other possible areas. Some scholars think that an error in copying, involving the changing of the letters ",{"c":["r"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (resh) to ",{"c":["w"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," (waw) was responsible for the origin of the name Hivite from Horite."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Others have suggested a scribal confusion of names, since Zibeon is called a Hivite in ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.36.2","r":"Gen.36.2","rawR":"Gen.36.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.36.2","value":"Gen.36.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Genesis 36:2"}," and a Horite in verses ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.36.20","r":"Gen.36.20","rawR":"Gen.36.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.36.20","value":"Gen.36.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"20"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.36.29","r":"Gen.36.29","rawR":"Gen.36.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.36.29","value":"Gen.36.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"29"},". In several cases the Septuagint gives “Horite” in place of the Masoretic Text “Hivite” (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.34.2","r":"Gen.34.2","rawR":"Gen.34.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.34.2","value":"Gen.34.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 34:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.9.7","r":"Josh.9.7","rawR":"Josh.9.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.9.7","value":"Josh.9.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 9:7"},"). Other passages in the Septuagint read “Hittite” rather than “Hivite” (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.11.3","r":"Josh.11.3","rawR":"Josh.11.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.11.3","value":"Josh.11.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 11:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Judg.3.3","r":"Judg.3.3","rawR":"Judg.3.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.3.3","value":"Judg.3.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 3:3"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The overlapping or equivalence of Hivite and Horite in ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.36.1-43","r":"Gen.36.1-43","rawR":"Gen.36.1-43","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.36.1-43","value":"Gen.36.1-43"}],"t":"ref","v":"Genesis 36"}," probably indicates some relationship between the two peoples (cf. Ishmaelites and Midianites in ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.37.27-28,36","r":"Gen.37.27-28,36","rawR":"Gen.37.27-28,36","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.37.27-28,36","value":"Gen.37.27-28,36"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 37:27-28, 36"},"). Perhaps both Horites and Hivites are related to the Hurrians, who are well attested archaeologically."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["The fact that there are some 25 occurrences of the name “Hivite(s)” in the OT, nearly one-third of which come in Joshua, makes it probable that they were a distinct people. Aside from Hivites in Palestine, they also appeared in Edomite territory (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.36.2","r":"Gen.36.2","rawR":"Gen.36.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.36.2","value":"Gen.36.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 36:2"},"). OT references to Hivites include Hamor (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.34.2","r":"Gen.34.2","rawR":"Gen.34.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.34.2","value":"Gen.34.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 34:2"},"), the men of Gibeon (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.9.7","r":"Josh.9.7","rawR":"Josh.9.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.9.7","value":"Josh.9.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 9:7"},"), the northern Hivites (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.3.3-8","r":"Judg.3.3-8","rawR":"Judg.3.3-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.3.3-8","value":"Judg.3.3-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 3:3-8"},"), and those who lived near Tyre (",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.24.7","r":"2Sam.24.7","rawR":"2Sam.24.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.24.7","value":"2Sam.24.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sm 24:7"},"). During the reign of Solomon, the Hivites and other foreign inhabitants of the land were made slaves; that is, they were put under forced labor (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.9.20-21","r":"1Kgs.9.20-21","rawR":"1Kgs.9.20-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.9.20-21","value":"1Kgs.9.20-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 9:20-21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.8.7","r":"2Chr.8.7","rawR":"2Chr.8.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.8.7","value":"2Chr.8.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 8:7"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Hivites","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Gn 10:17","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.10.17","rawQueryValue":"Gen.10.17","sourceRefExact":"Gen.10.17"},{"anchorText":"Jgs 3:3","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Judg.3.3","rawQueryValue":"Judg.3.3","sourceRefExact":"Judg.3.3"},{"anchorText":"Jos 11:3","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Josh.11.3","rawQu